Thanks @GromNaN. The `EnvelopeEncrypter` docblock now says that these backends throw `UnsupportedOperationException`, when encrypting and when decrypting.

I'd rather not add `context` to `#[Encrypted]`. Whether the name can be authenticated depends on the backend, not on the column. All `#[Encrypted]` columns use the same encrypter. With a sealed box and no store, they all need `context: false`. With a store, none of them does. So this belongs to the config, next to the KMS. Also, columns with the same key and type use the same type, so a flag on one column could contradict another column.

For `refusedContext()`, the backend contract says that a backend that can't enforce AAD must throw `UnsupportedOperationException`. A plain `RuntimeException` can also be a network error, or a Vault 403 when the token can't read `keys/<name>`. Telling people to set `context: false` would be the wrong advice there.

